    "evidence_text": "CD28 and ITK signals regulate autoreactive T cell trafficking\n\nWe propose that ITK specifically licenses autoreactive T cells to enter tissues to mount destructive immune responses.\n\nThis occurs earlier than and independently of tyrosine kinase activation.\n\nT cell receptor ligation induces the formation of dynamically regulated signaling assemblies\n\nLAT, Grb2, and Gads were transiently associated with the TCR.\n\nDirect observation of ligand recognition by T cells\n\nBasic residues in the T-cell receptor \u03b6 cytoplasmic domain mediate membrane association and modulate signaling.\n\nThe precise mechanism of T-cell triggering following TCR ligand engagement remains elusive.\n\nT cell receptor-proximal signals are sustained in peripheral microclusters and terminated in the central supramolecular activation cluster.\n\nOur studies reveal a role for F-actin in TCR signaling beyond microcluster formation.\n\nFull activation of the T cell receptor requires both clustering and conformational changes at CD3.\n\nMembrane association of the CD3\u03b5 signaling domain is required for optimal T cell development and function.\n\nThe TCR:CD3 complex transduces signals that are critical for optimal T cell development and adaptive immunity.\n\nAltered TCR signaling from geometrically repatterned immunological synapses.",
